# =============================================================================
# Trivy misconfiguration ignores.
# =============================================================================
# Location: ci/trivyignore (moved from the repo root).
# Consumed by .github/scripts/ci-iac-scan.sh:
#   trivy config --ignorefile ci/trivyignore \
#     --skip-dirs "**/examples/**" --skip-dirs "**/localstack/**" \
#     --format json --output <report> infra/terraform
# The verdict is NOT trivy's exit code — `agents.ci.ci_gate scan-gate
# --scanner trivy-config` reads the JSON and fails on any HIGH/CRITICAL that
# survives this file. The accepts below were triaged at that threshold.
#
# ID FORMAT IS LOAD-BEARING. trivy filters a misconfiguration when the ignore
# entry matches its `ID`, its `AVDID`, or one of its `Aliases`
# (pkg/result/filter.go). trivy 0.69.3 reports terraform misconfigurations with
# ID `AWS-0104` and an EMPTY AVDID, and the aliases are tfsec rule names
# ("aws-ec2-no-public-egress-sgr"), not AVD identifiers. The `AVD-`-prefixed IDs
# this file used to carry therefore matched nothing — they were inert. Use the
# bare `AWS-0nnn` form that appears in the report, and verify against
# ci-results/trivy-config-report.json rather than against the AVD website URL.
# =============================================================================
# Each ID below is an ACCEPTED finding for this single-account / single-EC2 /
# single-region architecture. Each maps ONLY to the resources noted, so a
# global ignore loses no real coverage today. These mirror the accepts already
# documented in ci/checkov.yaml and the inline checkov:skip annotations.

# AWS-0104 — Security group rule allows unrestricted egress to 0.0.0.0/0.
# The single EC2 host needs outbound internet on 443/80/53 to reach AWS APIs
# (Bedrock, ECR, SSM), OS/package updates, ACME/Let's Encrypt, and DNS. This is
# the documented residual in docs/_audit/tf_security.md (L-9). Tighter control
# would require AWS Network Firewall + domain allow-listing (out of scope for a
# single-EC2 design). Only the app SG egress rules in modules/network match
# (infra/terraform/modules/network/main.tf), reached from the foundation root:
# exactly four of that SG's five aws_vpc_security_group_egress_rule blocks use
# cidr_ipv4 = "0.0.0.0/0" — app_https, app_http, app_dns_udp, app_dns_tcp. The
# fifth (app_self) targets a referenced_security_group_id and never matched this
# check. Every other SG in the tree is already tighter: aws_security_group.
# endpoints has no egress rule at all, and aws_default_security_group.vpc_default
# is deliberately wiped empty.
#
# PARTIALLY REDUCIBLE, NOT ACCEPTED AS-IS FOREVER: only app_https and app_http
# genuinely need the whole internet (apt/OS updates, GitHub, ACME, OCSP, and the
# Bedrock/S3 public-endpoint fallbacks). The two DNS rules do not — the instance
# resolves through the VPC resolver, which is reachable inside the VPC CIDR, so
# 53/udp+tcp could be narrowed to the VPC CIDR and remove DNS-over-port-53 as an
# exfiltration channel. That is deliberately NOT done in the same change as the
# AWS-0136 fix: it is a live-traffic firewall change whose failure mode is total
# DNS loss on the host if anything on the box is pointed at an external resolver
# (a hard-coded resolv.conf entry, a Docker DNS override), and it would not clear
# this finding anyway because 80/443 keep it red. Tracked as its own change with
# its own verification.
AWS-0104

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT ignored — recorded so the absences are deliberate, not oversights.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0132 (S3 bucket without a customer-managed KMS key) WAS accepted here. The
# accept is withdrawn because its only match is gone: it matched exactly one
# resource, the ALB access-log bucket in modules/alb_internal (reached from the
# velocityai-alb root), and both were removed along with the internal-ALB layer.
# The accept was legitimate while it existed — ELB access logging supports only
# SSE-S3, and a CMK made log delivery fail with "The bucket uses an unsupported
# server-side encryption option" — but keeping a global ignore for a resource
# that no longer exists would silently accept the next bucket added without a
# CMK. The three remaining buckets (tfstate, backups, audit_trail) all use the
# project CMK, so the check is intentionally live.
#
# AWS-0136 (SNS topic without a customer-managed CMK) WAS accepted here. The
# accept is withdrawn because it was both inaccurate and masking a real defect.
#
# Inaccurate: it named "the monitoring alerts topic (modules/monitoring, reached
# from the app root)" as the only match. That topic — aws_sns_topic.alerts — has
# always carried kms_master_key_id = var.kms_key_arn (the project CMK) and never
# matched this check. The two topics that actually matched were never mentioned:
#   * aws_sns_topic.alerts_useast1 (modules/monitoring) — used alias/aws/sns.
#   * aws_sns_topic.alerts (the since-removed modules/alb_internal) — resolved
#     to no encryption at all, because velocityai-alb left kms_key_arn = "" and
#     the old expression fell back to null. Both that module and its root layer
#     have been deleted, so this second match no longer exists.
#
# Masking a defect: an AWS-managed SNS key cannot carry alarm notifications. Its
# key policy does not authorise CloudWatch to call kms:Decrypt /
# kms:GenerateDataKey and cannot be edited, so the us-east-1 billing alarm was
# transitioning to ALARM and then failing to publish. The accept's reasoning
# ("payload is only an account ID and a USD threshold") argued impact was low,
# which was true, and concluded a CMK was not worth it, which was wrong: a CMK is
# the only configuration in which that alarm notifies anyone.
#
# The surviving topics now use customer-managed keys, and their key policies grant
# cloudwatch.amazonaws.com kms:Decrypt + kms:GenerateDataKey* so the publish path
# works. The project CMK (modules/kms) gained the same grant, which it was
# missing — the eu-central-1 alerts topic was CMK-encrypted with a key policy
# that did not let CloudWatch use it, so all ~19 regional alarms were silently
# failing to notify as well. The check is intentionally live so that a future
# topic added without a CMK fails CI instead of being quietly undeliverable.
#
# AWS-0031 (ECR mutable tags) WAS accepted here on the grounds that a build
# retry needs to re-push the same tag. That accept is withdrawn: the repos now
# default to IMMUTABLE (infra/terraform/modules/ecr) and build retries are
# handled by reusing the existing image's digest instead of re-pushing it
# (.github/workflows/deploy.yml, "Probe ECR for an existing image"). The check
# is intentionally live so that flipping a repo back to MUTABLE fails CI. The
# only AWS-0031 hits today come from infra/terraform/localstack, which pins
# `image_tag_mutability = "MUTABLE"` on purpose for a throwaway local emulator
# and is excluded by --skip-dirs, not by an ignore — so the check still fires
# for shared/ and for the module's own example.
